You're assuming the character has acquired teleport intrinsic, which
isn't necessarily true (and is definately false in my case).

Another aspect that hasn't been explicitly considered is scrolls of
teleportation.  If one goes ^T, or uses a scroll when one can't ^T,
one isn't spending more than a few turns on any teleport eligible
level in gehennom.  Spending slightly more time on no-teleport levels
reduces the pressure to use a limited supply of teleport scrolls.
Only once both scrolls and spell energy is expended do random
teleports enter into the equation.

Whether walking or jumping through no-tele levels is better depends on
several factors:  Do I have any scrolls of teleport left?  Am I
relying on a ring or spell to teleport?  Am I below the Valley of the
Dead?  Do I have the Eye of the Aethiopica? If the answer at least two
of those questions is "yes", then IMO, walking through a no teleport
level is the smart play because it gets me higher in the dungeon
before random teleports even enter into the equation.

As I said, it's counterintuitive.

The only exception is nearly full spell energy.  Then jumping makes
sense regardless of where one is.

